# Poly Example: Closures
#
# A closure is an anonymous function value written `|params| body`. It can
# be stored in a variable, passed to functions, and called like any other
# function. Unlike a named `fn`, a closure can also capture (read) variables
# from the surrounding scope.
#
# This example shows the two common forms:
#   1. `|x: i32| x * x`  — an expression-body closure with a typed parameter.
#   2. `|n| n + factor`  — an untyped parameter that inherits its type, and a
#                          body that captures `factor` from the enclosing scope.

fn main()
    put "Closures Demo"

    # Form 1: a closure with an explicitly typed parameter stored in a
    # variable. The variable can then be called like a function.
    var square := |x: i32| x * x
    put "square(6):"
    put square(6)

    # Form 2: a closure that CAPTURES `factor` from the surrounding scope.
    # When `add_factor` is called later it still reads this `factor`, which
    # is what makes closures more flexible than plain function pointers.
    var factor := 10
    var add_factor := |n| n + factor
    put "add_factor(5):"
    put add_factor(5)

    # Closures can also be written inline at the call site of a function
    # that accepts a function-typed parameter (see the higher-order
    # functions example for the full pattern).
    put "inline closure:"
    put (|a: i32, b: i32| a * b)(3, 4)
end fn
